== Servants and Masters in the Apostle’s Words

=== §. 123. #emph[Of the subjection which believing servants owe.]

Because there is yet another order in the family besides those noted before, namely the order of masters and servants, the apostle also prescribes their duty to them.

As he began with wives and children in the two former orders, so here he begins with servants, who are the inferiors, for the same reasons #footnote[§. 10. Why the apostle is so full in urging servants to their duty.] given before.

The apostle is somewhat full in setting out the duties of servants, and in urging them to perform their duty; and that for two special reasons: one with respect to those whose masters were unbelievers, the other with respect to those whose masters were saints.

1\. In those days, when the #footnote[#emph[Seruus beneuolentia prosequatur dominum, quamuis impium. Qui fidelem habet dominum, saluo eius dominio, diligat vt patrem. Constit. Apost. li.] 4. #emph[ca.] 12. \[Let a servant attend his master with good will, even though he is ungodly. Let him who has a believing master love him as a father, his master’s authority kept intact.\] Gal 3:28.] gospel was first preached to the Gentiles, there were many servants converted by its preaching whose masters did not embrace the gospel. So those servants began to imagine that, being Christians, they ought not to be subject to their masters who were unbelievers.

2\. There were other servants whose masters believed the gospel as well as they did. Now because the gospel taught that “there is neither slave nor free … for you#super[+] are all one in Christ Jesus,” they thought that they ought not to be subject to their master, who was their brother in Christ.

The apostle hints at these two wrongheaded and presumptuous notions, and expressly answers them in #footnote[1 Tim 6:1, 2.] another place. And because they had taken too deep root in the minds of many servants, the apostle here in this place labors all the more earnestly to root them out, and that by pressing thoroughly on their conscience the subjection by which they are bound to their masters, as masters, whatever their masters’ disposition might be. More of this #footnote[Treatise 7, §. 2, 3.] later.

Here, by the way, note three points.

+ The gospel does not free inferiors from the subjection #footnote[1\. #emph[Observation.]] to men to which they are bound by the moral law.
+ Men are ready to turn the grace of God into liberty. #footnote[2\. #emph[Observation.]]
+ As errors begin to sprout up in the church, ministers #footnote[3\. #emph[Observation.]] must take care to root them out.

=== §. 124. #emph[Of the meaning of the fifth verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:5.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Servants, be obedient to those who are your#super[+] masters according to the flesh, with fear and trembling, in singleness of your#super[+] heart, as to Christ.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] title #emph[(servants)] is a general title, which may be applied #footnote[οἱ δοῦλοι.] to all who by any outward legal bond or right #footnote[#emph[Origo vocabuli seruorum in Lat. lingua inde creditur ducta, quod hi qui iure belli possint occidi à victoribus cùm seruabantur serui fiebant: à seruando appellati. Aug. de Ciu. Dei lib.] 19. #emph[cap.] 15. #emph[Vide Lud Viu. ibid.]] owe their service to another, whatever the sex of the persons themselves, and whatever the kind of their servitude, whether more servile or more free.

#emph[Servile,] as those who are born servants, or sold as servants, or taken in war, or ransomed. For in ancient times those who were taken in war and saved from death were called servants.

#emph[Free,] as those who are made servants by voluntary contract, whether at will, as some menservants, journeymen and laborers, or for a certain term of years, as apprentices, clerks and the like. Therefore, whatever the birth, parentage, standing or former condition of any may have been, being

#emph[Servants,] they #emph[must be subject, and do the duty of servants:] the #footnote[4\. #emph[Observation.]] apostle’s indefinite title #emph[(servants)] admits no exception of anyone.

The other title #emph[(masters)] has as large an extent, comprising #footnote[τοῖς κυρίοις. What masters are meant.] under it both sexes, #emph[masters] and #emph[mistresses,] and of these all sorts: great and lowly, rich and poor, strong and weak, believers and unbelievers, true professing Christians and the profane, superstitious, idolatrous and heretical persons, and the like; so that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[No condition or disposition of the master exempts a servant] #footnote[5\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[from performing his duty to him.]]

Among other degrees and differences, let it most especially be noted that both sexes, mistresses as well as masters, are meant here, so that the duties enjoined to be performed to masters may correspondingly be performed to mistresses (so far as they are common to both), and that both by maidservants and also by menservants who are under mistresses. In families mistresses are as common as masters, and therefore I thought it good to make special #emph[mention] of this.

Under this word #emph[(obey)] are included all the duties which #footnote[ὑπακούετε.] servants owe to their masters. It is the same word that was used before in the first verse, and it has as large an extent here, applied to servants, as it had there, applied to children. It shows that

#emph[The rule of servants] (as servants) #emph[is the will of their master.] #footnote[6\. #emph[Observation.] κατὰ σάρκα.]

This clause #emph[(according to the flesh)] is referred by some to the action of obedience, as if the apostle added it to show what kind of obedience servants owe to their masters, namely a secular, bodily obedience in temporal things, as opposed to the spiritual obedience which is due to God alone.

#emph[Answer.] Though a distinction may be made between the service which is due to God and that which is due to man, yet this application of the phrase in this place may give servants occasion to think that if they perform outward service to their masters, all is well, and they owe them no inward fear or honor. That is an error which the apostle here chiefly opposes.

But because this clause #emph[(according to the flesh)] is directly #footnote[Why masters are said to be after the flesh. #emph[Dominis carnalibus. vet. transl.]] joined to #emph[masters,] I refer it to the persons to whom obedience is to be given, and so take it as a description of them, as if he had said, to #emph[fleshly] or #emph[bodily masters.]

The apostle describes masters in this way for these reasons.

1\. For #emph[distinction:] to show that he means masters who are of the same mold as servants, so distinguishing them from God, who is a spirit. In the same way the apostle distinguishes between #emph[fathers of our flesh] and #emph[the Father of spirits.] #footnote[Heb 12:9.]

2\. To #emph[forestall an objection:] so that servants might not say, our masters are flesh and blood as we are, so why should we be subject to them? To counter that notion, the apostle expressly says that obedience is due to #emph[masters after the flesh.]

3\. For #emph[mitigation] of their servitude: for since their masters are flesh, they have no power except over the bodies of their servants; their servants’ spirits are free from them. In this respect the apostle calls Christian servants #emph[the Lord’s freemen.] #footnote[1 Cor 7:22.]

4\. For #emph[consolation] in their present condition, which lasts only for a time, because their masters are flesh: whatever is according to the flesh does not last long, but has its appointed end.

5\. For #emph[direction:] to show in what things especially the obedience which belongs strictly to a master consists, namely in secular, outward things. For everyone must be served according to his nature: as God, being spirit, must be served in spirit, so man, being flesh, must be served in the flesh. Now this service in the flesh is not opposed to sincere and upright service, but to spiritual service. So, by implication, what some take to be the chief meaning may also be intended.

#emph[Objection.] Masters may command spiritual things, namely to worship God, and in such and such a manner.

#emph[Answer.] On his own authority a master cannot command such things: there must be a higher warrant for doing them than the commandment of a man.

A main point intended here is this, that

#emph[Masters are not to be lightly regarded because they are after the] #footnote[7\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[flesh:] that is, weak, frail, short-lived, of the same nature as servants.

So that, on the strength of this description of masters, servants #footnote[Servants’ fear of their masters. μετὰ φόβου, καὶ τρόμου.] should not take too much heart to themselves, the apostle adds this clause #emph[(with fear and trembling, etc.),] which relates to the manner of their obedience. No slavish fear is meant here, as if servants should live in continual dread, or tremble at the sight of their masters. A servant may be brought to do so by the tyranny of some master, but to do so is no Christian duty. What the apostle requires here is a duty belonging to all Christian servants toward all sorts of masters, even the mildest there are. It is therefore an awe-filled respect for a master’s authority, and a dutiful reverence for his person, that is required here; and it is opposed to sauciness, impudence, boldness, defiance, talking back, grumbling and muttering against their masters, and other such vices. To show how foul those faults are, and what great respect servants ought to bear to their masters, these two words #emph[(fear] and #emph[trembling)] are joined together. In #footnote[Difference between #emph[fear] and #emph[trembling.]] effect they declare one and the same thing, yet for explanation’s sake they may be distinguished. For #footnote[φόβος. #emph[v.] 33.] #emph[fear] signifies a reverent respect for someone: it is what was required of wives in the previous chapter. Though the thing required of wives and of servants is in general the same, yet the particular manner and measure of a servant’s fear is far different.

#emph[Trembling]#footnote[τρόμος.] belongs more especially to servants: it is a dread of punishment. And it is required of servants, not as if they should do all things solely for fear of punishment, but because God has put a staff into a master’s hands, servants must tremble at the power their masters have, and fear to provoke them to strike. To this purpose the apostle says to subjects, with respect to the power which a magistrate has, “be afraid, for #footnote[Rom 13:3, 4.] he does not carry the sword in vain.”

From this learn that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[The authority of a master ought to strike a servant’s heart with] #footnote[8\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[dread.]]

The dread which servants ought to have of their master’s power and authority makes many care for no more than to avoid their master’s displeasure. Therefore the apostle adds a further degree of a servant’s subjection, namely that #footnote[ἐν ἁπλότητι τῆς καρδίας ὑμῶν.] it be #emph[in singleness of heart,] that is, honest, entire, upright: for this is opposed to hypocrisy, pretense and fraud. Indeed, it must be of YOUR#super[+] heart, not another’s. Another, in the simplicity of his heart, may think you do a thing better than you do, by putting the most charitable interpretation on everything; but if you do it in singleness of #emph[your own] heart, it will in truth be as it appears to be. So that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[All the services which servants perform for their masters must] #footnote[9\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[be done in truth and uprightness.]]

The apostle gives this direction to Christians, who have to do not only with masters according to the flesh (who only “see the outward appearance”) but also with the master of spirits, #footnote[1 Sam 16:7 ὡς τῷ Χριστῷ.] who “sees the heart.” And therefore he also added this clause, #emph[as to Christ,] teaching servants by it that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Servants, in their obedience to their masters, must approve] #footnote[10\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[themselves to Jesus Christ as well as to their masters after the flesh.]]

This phrase #emph[(as to Christ)] implies as much as the phrase #emph[(in the Lord)] of which we spoke #footnote[§. 96.] before.

=== §. 125. #emph[Of the meaning of the sixth verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:6.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Not with eye-service, as men-pleasers, but as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] verse and the one following are added as a further explanation of the last clause of the previous verse. It seems that servants, however they might in some measure perform the main duty of obedience, yet failed exceedingly in the manner of performing it. Because masters were only men, #emph[masters according to the flesh,] who neither had power over the heart nor could discern its disposition, servants thought they had done their duty well if they had outwardly performed what their master required. Now, to root out this bitter weed and to reform this corrupt notion, the apostle is fuller in making clear the point of sincere and upright service. Therefore, to express his mind and meaning more fully, he first lays down the vice contrary to the sincerity named above (for contraries laid side by side bring out one #footnote[Opposita iuxta se posita magis elucescunt.] another all the more), and then returns to declare the virtue more distinctly. From this note that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Those points are most to be urged on people in which they] #footnote[11\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[most fail.]]

The vice noted here as contrary to sincere service is called #footnote[ὀφθαλμοδουλεία.] #emph[eye-service.] Our English word answers the original properly and fitly, both in its derivation and in its true sense and meaning. It implies a merely outward service, only #footnote[What eye-service is.] to satisfy the eye of man.

And it is twofold:

- #emph[Hypocritical.]
- #emph[Fawning.]

#emph[Hypocritical] service is what is merely for show: when something is claimed to be done which in fact is not done; as if a servant should come from his master’s work all in a sweat, as if he had taken extraordinary pains at it, whereas he has done nothing at all, but has made himself sweat some other way, or only made a show of sweat.

#emph[Fawning] service is what is indeed done, but only in the master’s presence. Such servants are those who will be very diligent and faithful in doing the things their masters see, or that will come to their notice; but otherwise, behind their master’s back, and in things which they hope will never come to his knowledge, they will be as negligent and unfaithful as if they were no servants. Yet to satisfy their masters and to humor them, they will do anything, however unlawful.

From this vice, thus exposed, note that

#emph[God requires more than what may satisfy man’s eye.] #footnote[11\. #emph[Observation.]] God’s eye is a piercing eye, and can see much foulness where to man’s eye all things seem very fair. So they greatly deceive themselves who think all is well because no man can say to them, #emph[black is your eye.]

Those who content themselves with doing #emph[eye-service] the #footnote[Men-pleasers. ἀνθρωπάρεσκοι] apostle here calls #emph[men-pleasers;] and he gives them this title for two special reasons.

1\. To show the ground of #emph[eye-service:] which is that all their care is to please their master, who is a man. For they know well that man can see only the outward show, or what is done before his face.

2\. To show the heinousness of that sin: for it is tainted with atheism, in that the man guilty of it has no regard for God, but prefers his master before God. He does not care whether he pleases God, so long as he pleases his master. For this is the force of that word #emph[(men-pleasers):] it is spoken in opposition to God, as the apostle implies in another place, saying, “If I still pleased #footnote[Gal 1:10.] men, I would not be the servant of Christ.”

#emph[Objection.] How can it be so heinous a sin to be #emph[men-pleasers,] #footnote[Titus 2:9.] when the apostle advises servants to #emph[please] their masters #emph[in all things?]

1\. #emph[Answer.] The advice given there is not simply to please, but to #footnote[εὐαρέστους εἶναι.] #emph[please well,] as the original word properly signifies, and as the King James translators have fitly rendered it.

2\. #emph[Answer.] That general phrase #emph[(all things)] must be restricted #footnote[How masters are to be pleased in all things.] to the duties of a servant, and to all the parts of obedience which he mentions there in the words directly before. Servants therefore must please their masters in all things which their masters have power to require of them, and which they are bound to do. The men-pleasing spoken of here is opposed to the pleasing of God; the pleasing of men mentioned there is subordinate to our pleasing of God. Here, to please men is to flatter them in everything, good or evil; there, to please them is to obey them conscientiously in every lawful thing. Here is condemned a seeking to please men in the first place, and that only, and wholly in everything; whereas we ought first to seek God’s approval, then the testimony of a good conscience, and after these a pleasing of men, but in, for and under God. From this, then, observe that

#emph[A servant must not give himself wholly to flattering and pleasing] #footnote[12\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[his master:] for so he may in many things highly displease Almighty God.

To avoid the two sins named above, #emph[eye-service] and #emph[men-pleasing,] the apostle gives an excellent direction in these words, #emph[as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart.] Here we may observe that #emph[servants of Christ] are set against #emph[men-pleasers,] and #emph[doing God’s will from the heart] against #emph[eye-service.]

#emph[Servants of Christ] are those who know that their masters are in #footnote[δοῦλοι τοῦ Χριστοῦ.] Christ’s place, bear his image, have their authority from him, and are under him; so that in serving their masters they serve #footnote[Who are servants of Christ.] Christ. And so far as they can serve both together, they will. But if the two turn out to be contrary masters, so that one of necessity must be left, then they will hold fast to the highest master, who is Christ. In this respect they are called #emph[the Lord’s freemen,] 1 Cor 7:22. So we see how a servant may #footnote[#emph[Quo pacto servus non est servus? Cum propter Deum omnia facit. Chrys. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] be no servant, if he does all things for the Lord.

From this contrast between #emph[men-pleasers] and #emph[servants of Christ,] note that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Those who in all things give themselves to please men are no servants of Christ.]]

So that we may know better who are servants of Christ, #footnote[13\. #emph[Observation.]] the apostle adds a description of them in these words #emph[(doing the will of God from the heart).] Christ’s will is God’s will: for as Christ is God, the Father’s will and his are one and the same; as he is man, he orders his will wholly by his Father’s: #emph[he does not seek] #footnote[John 5:30.] #emph[his own will, but the will of the Father who sent him.]

The apostle adds this description of a servant of Christ partly as a direction to servants, to teach them how in serving their masters they may be servants of Christ (namely, by having an eye to God’s word, in which his will is revealed both for the matter and for the manner of all things they do), and partly as a motive to persuade them to be content with their place and to do their duty cheerfully, because such is the will of God.

#emph[God’s will is what must direct and settle everyone in the] #footnote[14\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[things they do:] for God’s will is the rule of what is right. Everything he wills is entirely right, and nothing is right that swerves from his will.

To put a difference between Christ and other masters, and to show that he does not look (as man does) on the outward appearance, but sees the heart, the apostle adds this clause #emph[(from the heart).] And it declares that

#emph[A good thing must be well done.] To do what is God’s #footnote[15\. #emph[Observation.]] will, commended by his word, is in substance a good thing; to do it from the heart is the right manner of doing it. What is good and is done in the right manner is #emph[well done.]

=== §. 126. #emph[Of the meaning of the seventh verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:7.]

    #text(style: "italic")[With good will doing service, as to the Lord and not to men.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

In this verse the apostle presses home yet again the point named above concerning servants’ manner of obeying their masters, and their care in it to approve themselves to their highest master. From this observe that

#emph[Matters that are needful and weighty are to be pressed again and again.] #footnote[16\. #emph[Observation.]] This is a needful point, because servants fail exceedingly in it; and it is a weighty point, because all the comfort and benefit of service consists in God’s approval. But the former point is not merely and barely repeated here, but set down in such a way that other good directions are given to servants #footnote[What it is to serve with good will.] for their manner of obedience.

1\. To serve #emph[with good will] is somewhat more than #emph[with singleness of heart.] For it further implies

1\. A readiness and cheerfulness in doing a thing: doing it with a #emph[good mind,] as the derivation of the Greek word #footnote[μετ’ εὐνοίας.] shows.

2\. A desire and endeavor that their masters may reap profit and benefit from their service, by which they show that they bear #emph[good will] and a #emph[good mind] toward their masters.

In setting down servants’ duties, the apostle uses a different word from the one in the fifth verse, namely this one #emph[(doing service),] #footnote[δουλεύοντες.] by which he shows that a servant’s place and duty is of a lowlier and more inferior kind than the place and duty of a child or a wife. The former word#footnote[ὑπακούετε.] #emph[(obey)] was common to all; this word#footnote[δουλεύοντες.] #emph[(doing service)] belongs only to servants, and the very title of a #footnote[δοῦλος.] #emph[servant] is derived from it. From this note that

#emph[Though wives and children are inferiors as well as servants, yet] #footnote[17\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[servants must not look for such privileges as they have.] Another manner of subjection must be performed by servants.

The clause added #emph[(as to the Lord)] is in effect the same #footnote[ὡς τῷ κυρίῳ.] as that in the fifth verse #emph[(as to Christ),] for by the #emph[Lord] he here means #emph[the Lord Christ.] But it is added to answer an unspoken objection. For servants might say, #emph[You require us to serve] #footnote[Objection.] #emph[our masters with good will; but what if they are hard-hearted, and do not regard our good will, but abuse our good mind?] The apostle #footnote[Answer.] gives them this answer: do not look so much to men and their reward as to God and his reward; serve men in and for the Lord, even as if you#super[+] were serving God, and so your#super[+] service will not be in vain. The eighth verse, which follows from this as an inference, shows that this is what the apostle intends here. Learn therefore that

#emph[An eye is to be cast on God even in the duties which we perform] #footnote[18\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[to men:] and that both for approval and for reward from God.

The negative clause which follows in these words #emph[(and not to men)] is not to be taken #emph[absolutely] (for then it would contradict the apostle’s main aim in this place) but #emph[comparatively,] in relation to God; and that in two respects.

1\. That service is not to be done #emph[only] to men.

2\. That service is not to be done to men #emph[in and for themselves.] Service must be done to God as well as to men; indeed, #emph[in the service] #footnote[19\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[which we do to men, we must serve God.] Men must be served for the Lord’s sake, because the Lord has commanded it, and because they bear the Lord’s image and stand in his place: in the Lord, and under the Lord.

From this full declaration of the #emph[manner] of doing service #footnote[Difference between servants of men and servants of Christ.] to masters, note the difference between those servants who are #emph[servants of men] and those who are #emph[servants of Christ.]

+ #emph[Those] do all for the eye. #emph[These,] all from the heart.
+ #emph[Those] seek to please men. #emph[These] do the will of God.
+ #emph[Those] do their service discontentedly. #emph[These,] cheerfully.
+ #emph[Those] do all out of self-love. #emph[These,] with good will.

=== §. 127. #emph[Of the meaning of the eighth verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:8.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Knowing that whatever good thing any man does, the same he will receive from the Lord, whether he is slave or free.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[Great] is the ingratitude of many masters: they will demand #footnote[Why servants are put in mind of God’s reward.] all the service that a poor servant can possibly do, but repay his pains meagerly; indeed, perhaps reward him very badly for it, giving him not sufficient food, clothing and lodging, but frowns, rebukes and blows. Now, to uphold servants in such straits, and to encourage them to do their duty whether their masters regard it or not, the apostle in this verse labors to raise their minds to God, and to show them that God regards them and will reward them sufficiently, so that

#emph[Servants’ labor will not be in vain in the Lord.] To press #footnote[20\. #emph[Observation.]] this encouragement on them all the more, he sets it down as a thing granted by all, so clear that none of them can be ignorant of it #emph[(knowing),] as if he had said, you#super[+] all know #footnote[εἰδότες.] well enough that what I now say is most true. From this note that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[God’s regard for faithful servants is so well known that none who] #footnote[21\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[have any understanding can be ignorant of it.]]

The apostle’s argument is drawn from the #emph[general to a particular,] #footnote[A genere ad speciem.] and the generality is noted in the #emph[thing done (whatever)] and in the #emph[person] who does it #emph[(any man).] But because the generality of the thing might be stretched too far, he adds this limitation #emph[(good);] and because the generality of the person might be restricted too much, he adds this explanation #emph[(whether he is slave or free).] This distinction is used because in those days many servants were slaves, both men and women. Now the apostle’s argument may be framed like this: #emph[Everyone, of whatever condition and rank he is, will be rewarded by God for every good thing he does, whether it is great or small. Therefore every servant will be rewarded by God for every good service.]

The recompense promised is set out in a concise phrase #emph[(the same he will receive),] meaning that he will receive #footnote[τοῦτο κομιεῖται.] a reward for the same. That phrase refers to the crop which a farmer receives from the grain he sowed, which is of the same kind as he sowed: #footnote[1 Cor 15:38.] the seed being wheat, the crop is of wheat; the #footnote[2 Cor 9:6.] seed being sown plentifully, the crop will be plentiful. To the same purpose this apostle says in another place, “Whatever a man sows, #footnote[τοῦτο καὶ θερίσει.] that he will also #footnote[Gal 6:7.] reap.” Now, to apply this: servants who by their faithful service bring honor and glory to God will receive honor and glory in return. If they ask from whom they will receive it, the apostle expressly answers, #emph[from the Lord.] For it is the Lord who #footnote[παρὰ τοῦ Κυρίου.] said, #footnote[1 Sam 2:30.] “I will honor those who honor Me.” God will not forget them, though their masters may.

From this verse, thus explained, I gather these particular observations concerning servants.

1\. #emph[Servants may and ought to apply to themselves general] #footnote[22\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[promises made to Christians.] Otherwise this general argument of the apostle would be to little purpose in this place.

2\. #emph[A Christian may be a slave:] for the apostle directs this encouragement to Christians, among whom he presupposes #footnote[23\. #emph[Observation.]] some to be #emph[slaves,] setting them against #emph[free men,] who were also servants.

3\. #emph[Faithful service performed to men is a good thing:] for the #footnote[24\. #emph[Observation.]] good things which servants especially do lie in their service.

4\. #emph[As God does not accept men because they are free, so neither] #footnote[25\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[does he reject them because they are slaves.] It is not the person, but the work, that he regards.

5\. #emph[The faithful service of servants is like good seed sown:] it #footnote[26\. #emph[Observation.]] will bring forth a good crop. The metaphor hinted at here implies as much.

6\. #emph[God is honored by the faithful service of servants:] this is #footnote[27\. #emph[Observation.]] hinted at by the application of God’s reward to them, for God honors none but those who honor him.

=== §. 128. #emph[Of the connection of masters’ duties with servants’.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:9.]

    #text(style: "italic")[And you#super[+] masters, do the same things to them, forbearing threatening, knowing that your#super[+] Master also is in heaven, and there is no respect of persons with Him.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[To] the duties of servants the apostle joins the duties of masters, saying, AND #emph[you#super[+] masters.] From this learn that

#emph[Masters are as much bound to duty as servants. A like doctrine was noted from the connection of parents’ duties with children’s: there you#super[+] may see this general point further enlarged,] #footnote[1\. #emph[Observation.]] §. 115.

1\. God’s law requires as much: for it expressly enjoins many duties on masters (as we shall see in the eighth treatise, which follows).

2\. So also does the law of nature, which has tied master and servant together by a mutual and reciprocal bond, of doing good as well as of receiving good.

3\. The law of nations also requires as much. For in all nations where there was ever any good government, and where wise and good laws were made, particular laws have been made about the duties of masters.

4\. So too does the law of equity. One good turn deserves another; therefore the apostle says to masters, “give your#super[+] servants what is just and equal.” #footnote[Col 4:1.]

Now let masters take note of this, and know that God, the great Lord of all, has made this relation between master and servant, and has set each of them in their separate and distinct places for the mutual good of one another, so that servants are no more for the good of masters than masters are for the good of servants. Therefore, as they look for duty, let them perform duty. If servants fail in their duty, let masters see whether they themselves are not the cause of it, by failing in theirs. Their authority will be no excuse before Christ, but a means to make their fault heavier and increase their condemnation: for the greater the talent, the more diligence is expected, and the stricter the account that will be demanded.

=== §. 129. #emph[Of the meaning of this phrase,] Do the same things.

These two titles #emph[(masters, servants)] are taken here as they were #footnote[§. 124.] before in the fifth verse.

All the duties of masters are included under this phrase, #emph[do the same things,] which at first sight may seem somewhat #footnote[τὰ αὐτὰ ποιεῖτε.] strange. For some may say: the things which servants must do are these, #emph[to fear, to obey, to do service,] and the like; and are masters to do #emph[the same things?]

#emph[Answer.] 1. These words are not to be referred to the particular duties which belong only to servants, but to the general rules of equity which are common to masters as well as servants; namely, that in their own places, #emph[with singleness of heart, as to Christ, not with eye-service, as men-pleasers, but as the servants of Christ, they do the will of God from the heart.]

2\. These words may be referred to the eighth verse, the verse immediately before, which lays down a general rule for all men in their own places to do the good things of their places. Now then, as servants must have an eye to their places, to do the good things of them, so masters must do #emph[the same things:] that is, #emph[they] must have an eye to #emph[their] places, to do the good things of them.

3\. These words may be taken without reference to any words before, and explained as a mutual, reciprocal and proportionate duty that ought to pass between master and servant: not in the particulars, as if the same duties were to be performed by each of them, for that would overthrow the order and degrees which God has set between master and servant, cross God’s ordinance, and imply a contradiction; but in general, that duties are to be performed by each to the other. In this respect the apostle said #footnote[§. 3.] before of all sorts, superiors and inferiors, “Submit to one another.” And so by this phrase the doctrine mentioned before is confirmed, that #emph[masters are as much bound to duty as servants.]

None of these answers contradicts another, but all of them may well be admitted, and all of them stand well together. They all imply a common equity between masters and servants, but no equality: mutual duties, but different and distinct duties, belonging to their separate places. Compare with this text what the apostle himself has noted more plainly and fully (Col 4:1), and we shall see him explain his own meaning: for what he implies here under this phrase #emph[(the same things)] he expresses there under these two words, #emph[just, equal.] Of these we shall #footnote[Treatise 8, §. 20, 44.] speak more distinctly hereafter.

The apostle purposely wraps up masters’ duties in this general phrase #emph[(the same things)] to forestall a secret objection raised from the eminence and superiority of masters over servants, which makes them think that servants exist only for the use of masters, and that masters are in no way bound to their servants. But if in general masters must do #emph[the same things,] then they exist for their servants’ good, as well as servants for theirs.

=== §. 130. #emph[Of masters forbearing threatening.]

The apostle in these words #emph[(forbearing threatening)] does #footnote[ἀνιέντες τὴν ἀπειλήν.] not absolutely forbid all manner of threatening, but only prescribes a moderation of it; and #footnote[Or, moderating.] the King James translators have expressed this much well in the margin beside this text. Threatening is a duty which masters ought to use as occasion requires, and that to prevent blows. But men in authority are naturally prone to lord it over their inferiors, and to think that they cannot show their authority except by harshness. For this reason the apostle warns husbands against #footnote[Col 3:19.] #emph[bitterness,] and parents against #footnote[Eph 6:4.] #emph[provoking their children to wrath.] Besides, the #footnote[See Treatise 8, §. 14.] Gentiles and heathen thought that they had an absolute power over servants, even of life and death; and so the Roman emperors made laws to restrain that severity, for such masters would use their servants like beasts. Now, so that Christian masters might not be of the same mind, the apostle exhorts them to #emph[forbear threatening.] From this note that

#emph[Authority must be moderated and kept within bounds:] otherwise #footnote[2\. #emph[Observation.] Authority to be kept within bounds.] it will be like a swelling river without banks and walls.

#emph[Threatening] is put here for every kind of severity, whether in heart, look, words or actions: for it is usual in Scripture to put one instance for all of the same kind.

#emph[Forbearing] implies a restraint of every kind of excess, as

1\. In time and continuance: when there is nothing but continual threatening on every small and slight occasion.

2\. In measure: when threatening is too fierce and violent, so that it makes the heart swell, and, as it were, fire come out of the eyes and thunder out of the mouth, and the body shake in every part.

3\. In carrying it out: when every vengeance once threatened is sure to be carried out, however sorry the party who caused the threatening may be for his fault, and though he humbles himself, and promises amendment, and gives good hope of it. Woe to us, the servants of the high God, if he dealt with us like that!

Here note that #emph[men may go to excess in doing a bounden duty,] and #footnote[3\. #emph[Observation.] Excess in duty not good.] so turn a needful virtue into a harmful vice. Great care therefore must be taken over the manner of doing good and lawful things.

Further, for the extent of this prohibition, we are to know that under the vice forbidden the contrary virtues are commanded, such as mildness, gentleness, patience, long-suffering and the like.

=== §. 131. #emph[Of masters’ subjection to a greater master.]

The latter part of this verse contains a reason to enforce the directions in the former part. The reason, in sum, lays down the subjection in which masters are under God: a point of which none of them could be ignorant, and therefore he sets it down in this way, #emph[knowing:] for #footnote[εἰδότες.]

#emph[All men know that there is one higher than the highest on earth.] #footnote[4\. #emph[Observation.]] The light of nature reveals as much; no pagan, much less a Christian, can be ignorant of it. In telling masters, when he speaks to them, that they have a master, he gives them to understand that

#emph[Those who are in authority are also under authority:] masters #footnote[5\. #emph[Observation.] 1 Tim 6:15. Gen 50:19.] have a master. For God is “Lord of lords,” master of masters. In this respect Joseph, a great governor, says, “Am I not under God?”

These two little words #emph[(even your#super[+],] or #emph[your#super[+] also)] add some #footnote[καὶ ὑμῶν.] emphasis, referring to servants, as if he had said: your#super[+] master as well as your#super[+] servants’ master. Some Greek copies, for greater clarity, read it like this: #emph[(both your#super[+] and their master).] #footnote[καὶ ὑμῶν, καὶ αὐτῶν.] The sense is the same whichever way we read it. It shows that in relation to God,

#emph[Masters and servants are in the same subjection,] and under a #footnote[6\. #emph[Observation.] Matt 23:8; 18:28.] like command. There is one master, even Christ; and all men, whoever they are, are #emph[brethren, fellow servants.]

=== §. 132. #emph[Of God’s being in heaven.]

That great master, under whom all masters on earth are, is said here to be #emph[in heaven,] to commend and set forth his dignity and authority all the more, and to make masters stand in greater awe of him. To a like purpose David, having set forth God #footnote[Ps 2:4, 10, 11.] sitting in #emph[the heavens,] draws from it this exhortation to the great rulers on earth: “Therefore be wise, O kings; be admonished, O judges of the earth. Serve the #smallcaps[Lord] with fear, and rejoice with trembling.”

#emph[Objection.] This placing of God #emph[in heaven] makes those who do not fear God all the more insolent and complacent: for they will be ready to think and say, “What does God know? Does He judge through #footnote[Job 22:13, 14.] thick darkness? Thick clouds veil Him so He does not see us as He traverses the vault of heaven.”

#emph[Answer.] 1. The apostle wrote to Christian masters, who thought better of God than such atheists did.

2\. The placing of God #emph[in heaven] does not confine him within its bounds: for #footnote[1 Kgs 8:27.] #emph[heaven, and the heaven of heavens, cannot contain him.] #footnote[Jer 23:23.] #emph[He fills heaven and earth.] #footnote[Matt 5:34, 35.] #emph[Though heaven is his throne, yet the earth also is his footstool.] But because the Lord manifests his glory most in heaven and from heaven, therefore #footnote[κατ’ ἐξοχήν.] #emph[by way of preeminence] he is said to be in heaven; and that in three special respects.

1\. To show that there is no proportion between him and earthly masters, however great they are. For as the heaven is higher than the earth, so God is more excellent, indeed infinitely more excellent, than any man. “Who is like the #smallcaps[Lord] our God, #footnote[Ps 113:5.] the One enthroned on high?” There is no such difference between masters and servants on earth.

2\. To show that he has his eyes continually on all his servants: he sees everything they do, as one placed above others sees all who are under him. #footnote[Ps 102:19.] “The #smallcaps[Lord] gazes out from heaven to earth.” #footnote[Ps 33:13.] “The #smallcaps[Lord] looks down from heaven; He sees all the sons of men.” #footnote[Prov 15:3.] “The eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are in every place, observing the evil and the good.” So this phrase shows the very opposite of what was objected before by wicked #emph[atheists.]

3\. To show that he is almighty: able both to reward his faithful servants (which is why David says, “I lift up #footnote[Ps 123:1.] my eyes to You, the One enthroned in heaven”) and also to execute vengeance on those who are unfaithful to God and cruel to their servants (which is why Solomon says, “If you see oppression,” #footnote[Eccl 5:8.] etc., “do not be astonished; for He who is higher than the highest regards it”).

From this placing of God #emph[(in heaven)] we learn these lessons.

1\. #emph[The eye of faith is needed to behold God,] for heaven #footnote[7\. #emph[Observation.] Heb 11:27.] is too high for any bodily eye to pierce. But by faith Moses “saw Him who is invisible.”

2\. #emph[Though masters had none on earth above them, yet there is] #footnote[8\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[one higher than they.] There is a master in heaven.

3\. #emph[Those who cannot get a hearing on earth still have one to appeal] #footnote[9\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[to.] There is a master in heaven.

4\. #emph[The command under which earthly masters stand is far] #footnote[10\. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[greater than the command they have:] for their commander is in heaven.

=== §. 133. #emph[Of God’s having no respect of persons.]

The apostle further adds of God, the great master of all, that #emph[with him there is no respect of persons.] The Hebrew word used to set forth this point signifies #footnote[פנים] #emph[a face;] so also does the Greek word translated here #footnote[πρόσωπον.] #emph[person:] it signifies both #emph[face] and #emph[person.] Now we know that the face of a man is outward, and is what, of all his parts, makes him most attractive in another’s eye. It is opposed to what is inward, namely the heart. In this respect it is said that “the #smallcaps[Lord] does not see as #footnote[1 Sam 16:7.] man does. For man sees the outward appearance, but the #smallcaps[Lord] sees the heart.” Here, by a #emph[synecdoche, face] or #emph[person] is put for every outward quality, state or condition which makes one preferred before another in man’s approval, such as beauty, comeliness, stature, wealth, honor, authority and the like. Now since God does not #emph[receive] or #emph[respect] persons, it shows that God does not prefer any one before another for any of the outward things named above, or any others like them. Elihu plainly explains this phrase in these words: “He does not accept the person of princes, nor favor #footnote[Job 34:19.] the rich over the poor.” The phrase is taken from those who sit on thrones of judgment, whose eyes should be blinded so that they cannot see the face or person of those who are brought before them, but only hear the cause.

This is aptly noted of God here, to counter a vain notion of many masters, who, though they know that God is their master as well as their servants’ master, yet think that God will not call them to account so strictly, but will bear with and tolerate them, because they are of a higher rank and in a better condition than servants. But by this phrase the apostle shows that

#emph[To God all are alike:] he makes no difference between any. #footnote[11\. #emph[Observation.]] He will show favor to the lowliest as well as to the greatest; he will take vengeance on the greatest as well as on the lowliest.

Many good lessons may be learned from this; among others, these:

1\. The poorest and lowliest there are may have as free access to God as the wealthiest and greatest, and their petition will be received as soon.

2\. The great ones on earth have as great cause to fear the avenging hand of God for any sin as the lowly.

3\. It befits magistrates and all in authority to conduct themselves impartially toward all who are under them: for they are in #emph[God’s place.] Respect of persons is the cause of all the injustice and wrong which magistrates do.

4\. It befits ministers to be faithful #emph[in all God’s house,] and with an #emph[even hand] to sow the seed of God’s word, and to keep themselves pure from the blood of #emph[all men:] for they are God’s stewards and ambassadors, and therefore they must have no respect of persons.
